## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of per-tenant isolation, backpressure, and queueing strategies under load, a common real-world challenge in llm-ops gateways.

## Key Concepts
- Priority queues and scheduling (WFQ, weighted RR)
- Backpressure and saturation handling
- Tenant isolation and SLA guarantees
- Observability: latency, tail latency, fairness

## Code Example
```javascript
class Scheduler {
  constructor() {
    this.queues = {Gold: [], Silver: [], Bronze: []};
  }
  enqueue(tenant, priority, job) {
    this.queues[priority].push({tenant, job});
  }
  dequeue() {
    for (const p of ['Gold','Silver','Bronze']) {
      if (this.queues[p].length) return this.queues[p].shift();
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and ensure Gold latency bounds under burst?
- How would you adapt weights if Bronze saturates frequently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses practical enterprise RAG architecture with multi-tenant isolation, policy governance, and regulatory compliance across global regions. It tests understanding of data locality requirements, policy lifecycle management, and comprehensive testing strategies—critical components for production LLM deployments serving Fortune 500 clients.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with tenant isolation
- Region-aware routing and data locality enforcement
- Policy versioning with TTLs and automated eviction
- Vector store segmentation and encryption
- Compliance testing (GDPR/CCPA) and drift detection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: enforce tenant-scoped lookups and region routing
function routeQuery(req) {
  const { tenantId, region } = req;
  const policyVersion = getCurrentPolicyVersion(tenantId);
  const vectorStore = getTenantVectorStore(`${tenantId}_${region}`);

  if (!isCompliantWithPolicy(req, policyVersion)) {
    throw new PolicyViolationError();
  }

  return vectorStore.search(req.query, {
    tenantId,
    policyVersion,
    dataMinimization: true
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Why: tracing + data lineage is critical for multi-tenant privacy and debugging; tests basic telemetry thinking.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry, W3C trace context
- Data lineage, tenantId propagation
- PII redaction in logs and policy checks
- Cross-region privacy and data locality

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: propagate tenantId in trace context and redact logs
import { trace, context } from '@opentelemetry/api';
function logWithTenant(tenantId, msg) {
  const span = trace.getSpan(context.active());
  span.setAttribute('tenant.id', tenantId);
  const redacted = redactPII(msg);
  console.log(`${tenantId} ${redacted}`);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate latency impact of redaction in production
- How would you test cross-service trace propagation with tenant drift
